   "text": "This SMILES is my best reading, and one part of the drawing is uncertain. The central core is drawn in a distorted, perspective-like way, so I resolved its bonding by chemical sense and the closest known relatives. Those are rifamycin-type compounds, specifically a benzoxazino-rifamycin with an isobutylpiperazine on the benzo ring. I took the stereocentres and double-bond geometry of the ansa chain from the rifamycin (rifampicin) pattern. I did not check each centre independently against the drawn wedges and hashes.\n\n**Ansa chain (left and top of the macrocycle)**\n- Starting from the amide: C(=O)\u2013C(CH3)=CH\u2013CH=CH\u2013CH(CH3)\u2013CH(OH)\u2013CH(CH3)\u2013CH(OH)\u2013CH(CH3)\u2013CH(OAc)\u2013CH(CH3)\u2013CH(OMe)\u2013CH=CH\u2013O.\n- The chain ends on a quaternary spiro carbon that carries a methyl group.\n- The acetate is written as OC(=O)C and the methoxy as OC.\n\n**Core (bottom right)**\n- The spiro carbon sits in a five-membered furan ring fused to the aromatic ring.\n- That aromatic ring carries an OH and a CH3.\n- A second OH sits on the carbon next to the spiro carbon.\n- The ring fused beyond it carries the amide NH and a C=O.\n- An oxazine ring (O and N=) joins this ring system to a cyclohexadienone, which is the quinone-imine part.\n- A piperazine bearing an isobutyl group (N\u2013CH2\u2013CH(CH3)2) is attached to the cyclohexadienone ring.\n\n**Uncertain point:** which core carbons bear the OH groups and the C=O.\n\nSMILES: C[C@H]1/C=C/C=C(\\C(=O)NC2=C3OC4=CC(N5CCN(CC(C)C)CC5)=CC(=O)C4=NC3=C6C2=O)... \n\nCorrected final SMILES below (the line above was cut off while drafting):\n\nSMILES: C[C@H]1/C=C/C=C(\\C(=O)NC2=C3OC4=CC(N5CCN(CC(C)C)CC5)=CC(=O)C4=NC3=C6C(C2=O)=C(O)C(C)=C7C6=C(O)[C@@](C)(O/C=C/[C@@H]([C@H]([C@H]([C@@H]([C@@H]([C@@H]([C@H]1O)C)O)C)OC(=O)C)C)OC)O7)/C"
